From 3dae92254572ca9973fa0890b2d0cce6a807b8cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hanbang <hanbang@localhost>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 00:54:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] WonderSwan UART: accurate status, timing and level IRQs,
 plus a link port

Rewrite comm.c so two consoles can be wired together by a frontend:
- 0xB3 status reports receive-ready (bit 0) and overrun (bit 1) whenever
  they are true; the old code hid bit 0 unless control bit 5 was set,
  which games that finish their init with 0x80 never do.
- A byte takes 10 bit times (3200 CPU cycles at 9600 bps, 800 at 38400)
  counted from the cycle it was written; only then is the transmitter
  empty again and the byte handed out through Comm_LinkTake().
- Comm_LinkGive() delivers a partner byte; a byte arriving while the
  receive buffer is full sets overrun and is dropped; a byte arriving
  while the UART is off is lost.
- Turning the UART off flushes the receive buffer and overrun, but a
  byte already being sent still goes out. One Piece Grand Battle Swan
  Colosseum depends on both: it toggles the UART to discard stale
  handshake bytes, and disables it right after writing a final reply.
- Serial send (IRQ 0) is now level-triggered like serial receive.
- All UART state, including the in-flight byte, is in the savestate.
- Optional stderr tracing of 0xB1/0xB3 and serial IRQs for testing.

---
